<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dharmasraya</h1><p class="st-meta">1183 CE – 1347 CE (approx.) · capital Dharmasraya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Malayu kingdom of the upper Batang Hari in Sumatra, successor to Srivijaya's power in the 12th and 13th centuries and partner of Javanese Singhasari.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> weakened, power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> shifted to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/melayu-kingdom">Malayu</a> kingdom on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batang-hari-river">Batang Hari River</a>. The Grahi inscription of 1183, found in southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thailand">Thailand</a>, names Srimat Trailokyaraja Maulibhusana Warmadewa as its overlord, showing its reach beyond Sumatra. Its centre, Dharmasraya, lay in today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sumatra">West Sumatra</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Dharmasraya controlled the gold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minangkabau-people">Minangkabau</a> highlands and the river route to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>. In 1275 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertanegara">Kertanegara of Singhasari</a> sent the Pamalayu expedition, and in 1286 a statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghapasa">Amoghapasa</a> was sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tribhuwanaraja-mauli-warmadewa">Tribhuwanaraja Mauli Warmadewa</a>, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padang-roco-inscription">Padang Roco inscription</a> records. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajrayana">Tantric Buddhism</a> flourished at the court.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1347 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adityawarman">Adityawarman</a>, a prince with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/melayu-kingdom">Malayu</a> and Javanese ancestry who had served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, inscribed the back of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghapasa">Amoghapasa</a> statue and declared himself king. He moved the court inland, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pagaruyung-kingdom">Pagaruyung Kingdom</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dharmasraya.jpg" alt="Dharmasraya" width="320" height="414"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amoghapasa_Padang_Roco_Inscription_Back.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1183 CE – 1347 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dharmasraya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Trailokyaraja Maulibhusana Warmadewa, Tribhuwanaraja Mauli Warmadewa, Adityawarman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Malayu-Dharmasraya, Kingdom of Malayu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1265&amp;focus=dharmasraya" aria-label="Dharmasraya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7496 2217 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dharmasraya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7816 2473L7833 2434M7816 2473L8114 2731M7816 2473L8112 2721M7816 2473L7794 2450"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7833 2434h0M8114 2731h0M8112 2721h0M7794 2450h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7816 2473h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7816 2473h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dharmasraya. <a href="/atlas?year=1265&amp;focus=dharmasraya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
